---
epr-meta-version: 1
id: native-reconciliation-governance
covers: subtree
purpose: >
  The native CLI admits local evidence and derives context over shared repository bytes.
  Acceptance-aware reconciliation serves agents choosing remaining work without promoting
  implementation reports or technical approvals into independently accepted experience.
  Its method and admission decisions are registered in seam-registry.yaml; local actor
  claims establish accountable attribution, not cryptographic peer authority.
rules:
  - id: developer-git-adapter-boundary
    class: inject
    when:
      write: "*.rs"
      contains-any: ["git::", "update-ref", "source_commit", "target_commit"]
    dedupe-of: ".epr-meta/manifest.md (Native developer tooling — composition and authority)"
    why: >
      Keep Git interpretation in an explicit Brit-facing adapter. Reuse the existing
      acceptance evaluator and records; revalidate exact source/target binding at the
      mutation boundary. Do not move Git semantics into EPRFS core or create another
      governance ledger. Update this crate's seam-registry.yaml and adapter journey
      tests when that boundary changes. The monorepo root records the composition;
      this advisory does not mechanically prove architectural separation.
    retire-when: >
      when dependency boundaries and executable adapter contracts enforce this ownership
      without an authoring reminder
  - id: reconciliation-fitness-review
    class: inject
    when:
      write: "*.rs"
      contains-any: ["reconciliation", "acceptance", "appoint"]
    route-to: { dest: code-reviewer }
    why: >
      Changes to acceptance admission or remaining-work interpretation require independent
      technical review and a separately appointed agent exercising native context against
      the declared intent. Preserve evidence, uncertainty, scope and algorithm identity;
      tests alone cannot establish fitness. Register changed decisions in seam-registry.yaml
      and record observed limitations in the acceptance report. Generic stock fulfillment
      remains a separate measure and cannot silently become experiential acceptance.
    retire-when: >
      when the authoring workflow attaches independent technical review and appointed
      experiential acceptance to every change of these decision surfaces by construction
---

# Native context and acceptance

One append-only local record supplies separate fulfillment, technical-review and acceptance
facts. The reader is a rebuildable interpretation with a declared version, bounded scope and
inspectable evidence. Reconciliation cannot erase adverse evidence or infer acceptance from
matching labels. Revalidation and contested states preserve what another agent must investigate.

This is a local proof of governance over bytes. Crossing the peer boundary requires independent
receiver verification and earned authority; an appointment in this checkout cannot confer them.

The native developer through-line reuses `epr flow` for intent, commitment, actor
attribution, review, acceptance and gates. A developer journal in brit is an adapter
to these existing records, not a second ledger. Shared flow and reconciliation
semantics do not interpret Git index/OIDs/worktrees or elect a release from storage
possession. Repository-specific CLI adapters do interpret Git: the integration
adapter being qualified binds existing acceptance to exact source/target revisions,
runs gates and revalidates before an atomic ref update. Keep that interpretation
in the adapter, outside shared governance and EPRFS core. Brit owns the developer
journey even while delegating execution to installed `epr`; this arrangement does
not yet deliver the one-executable target.

The monorepo [composition contract](../../../.epr-meta/manifest.md#native-developer-tooling--composition-and-authority)
links the independent owners and distinguishes authoring advisories, executable
checks and review obligations. Changes to this adapter's boundary must keep that
contract, this manifest and the existing decision registry coherent.

`covers: subtree` claims the whole `elohim-epr-cli` crate: the `epr` sources under `src/` (where
`src/.epr-meta` adds the recall-seam rule) and the integration tests under `tests/`. Those tests
exercise the `epr flow` verbs, and their `*_parity` gates hold the native readers to the recorded
outputs of the Python tools they replace.
